    {
      "id": "hdq1l3y",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Characteristics of Plants"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Plants are autotrophic eukaryotic organisms divided into Non-Vascular (Thallophyta, Bryophyta) and Vascular (Pteridophyta, Gymnosperms, Angiosperms) plants.",
        "Plant cells possess a rigid cell wall composed of cellulose, a large central vacuole, and plastids including chloroplasts.",
        "Plant tissues are classified into Meristematic (dividing) tissues and Permanent tissues (Simple: Parenchyma, Collenchyma, Sclerenchyma; Complex: Xylem, Phloem).",
        "Photosynthesis utilizes carbon dioxide, water, light energy, and chlorophyll to synthesize glucose and release oxygen as a byproduct.",
        "Plant hormones (phytohormones) like Auxins, Gibberellins, Cytokinins, Ethylene, and Abscisic acid regulate growth, development, and stress responses."
      ],
      "id": "7jb50vx"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gpmqu42",
      "content": "• Plants are multicellular eukaryotic organisms."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "muki3yp",
      "content": "• They contain chlorophyll and perform photosynthesis."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wz473a2",
      "content": "• Cell walls are made of cellulose."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k3lxvj6",
      "content": "• They are generally non‑motile."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kztx7io",
      "content": "• They store food mainly in the form of starch."
    },
    {
